.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:1747:4. When rework returns to `to-review`, spawn a new reviewer. Repeat until the reviewer accepts and the board item is `done`.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1748-5. If any child exits before its role handoff or leaves the item parked in `analysis`, `development`, `testing`, or `reviewing`, inspect the run and outcomes, then retry or reroute through a focused child. Do not hand routine recovery to the human.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1749-6. Continue dependent work and close eligible parents. Report completion only after accepted `done` is real on the board.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1750-
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:1751:Never ask the human to perform routine code, test, documentation, research, or acceptance review. Human review is valid only when a task explicitly defines a human approval boundary. Requested changes, failed tests, recoverable child exits, and retryable runtime failures are orchestration work: retry, reroute, or spawn focused rework and keep going.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1752-
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1753-## Stop-The-Line: No Forced Fits
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1754-
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1755-Do not keep implementing when the task starts requiring a forced fit. A forced fit is any path where the agent has discovered that the requested behavior conflicts with a platform/API constraint, product decision, UX state model, ownership boundary, or architecture, and the remaining "solution" is mostly compensating hacks.
--
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1760-
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1761-When this happens:
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1762-- Stop changing product code before adding another workaround layer.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1763-- Record the constraint, evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, recommended option, and exact decision or external input needed on the board as notes or an outcome resource.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:1764:- Mark the task `blocked` and request only that exact decision or external input. Do not use `blocked` for recoverable failures or ordinary rework.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1765-- Resume only after the product/architecture decision is explicit, or after you find a genuinely clean design that does not depend on the broken assumption.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1766-
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log-1767-Tests and stubs are not proof that a forced-fit design is correct. They are useful only after the state model and platform assumptions are valid.
.task-board/.resources/TASK-260715-89h7cw/TASK-260715-89h7cw_spawn-log_-implementer--developer--codex-.log:1768:Routine review, rework, test failures, recoverable child-process failures, and implementation choices resolvable from existing requirements are not reasons to hand control back to the human.
